/**
  @page examples-shapeoptimization-simple_cantilever Shape optimization of a cantilever
  @brief Compute and inspect a boundary-variation compliance descent.

  A cantilever carries a prescribed load while part of its boundary is
  clamped. The design task is to redistribute its material boundary so that
  elastic compliance and material area have a smaller weighted sum. This
  tutorial follows the state solve, regularized descent and mesh update in
  `SimpleCantilever2D`.

  @tableofcontents
  @m_footernavigation

  @section examples-shapeopt-sc-intro Objective and design boundary

  The elastic body occupies a planar domain @f$\Omega@f$. Its boundary has
  a clamped part @f$\Gamma_D@f$, a loaded part @f$\Gamma_N@f$ and an
  optimizable traction-free part @f$\Gamma_0@f$. The support and loaded
  boundary are held fixed during the design update. The objective is
  @f[
    J(\Omega)=C(\Omega)+\ell|\Omega|,\qquad
    C(\Omega)=\int_\Omega\sigma(u):\varepsilon(u)\,dx.
  @f]
  A fixed positive weight @f$\ell=5@f$ penalizes material area. The program
  performs 50 boundary-displacement updates with remeshing. The measured
  history describes those iterations; an optimality or volume-constraint
  stopping criterion would require additional controls.

  Compliance measures the work done by the applied load. Testing the elastic
  state equation with its displacement gives
  @f$C(\Omega)=\int_{\Gamma_N}f\cdot u\,ds@f$; the stored elastic strain
  energy is @f$C/2@f$. A smaller compliance means a stiffer response for this
  fixed load. The area term assigns a cost to retaining material, so both
  contributions must be inspected when interpreting a decrease in @f$J@f$.

  @subsection examples-shapeopt-sc-fields State and design fields

  @m_class{m-scroll}

  @parblock
  | Quantity | C++ storage | Role |
  | :--- | :--- | :--- |
  | @f$u@f$ | `u.getSolution()` | Elastic displacement under the current load |
  | @f$\theta@f$ | `g.getSolution()`, also referenced by `dJ` | Regularized direction for changing the design geometry |
  | @f$\Omega@f$ | `Omega` | Current mesh on which both fields are constructed |
  @endparblock

  The elastic displacement describes the loaded body's response. The design
  direction changes the domain on which the next elastic state is solved.

  @section examples-shapeopt-sc-setup Input and parameters

  @subsection examples-shapeopt-sc-setup-includes Modules

  The program uses local vector-valued `P1` spaces, elasticity assembly,
  sparse direct solves, MMG remeshing and XDMF output.

  @subsection examples-shapeopt-sc-setup-params Physical and numerical data

  @m_class{m-scroll}

  @parblock
  | Parameter | Value | Role |
  | :--- | :--- | :--- |
  | @f$\lambda@f$ | 0.5769 | First Lamé coefficient |
  | @f$\mu@f$ | 0.3846 | Shear modulus |
  | @f$\ell@f$ | 5 | Area penalty |
  | @f$h_{\max}@f$ | 0.1 | MMG upper size setting |
  | @f$h_{\min}@f$ | 0.01 | MMG lower size setting and displacement scale |
  | @f$\alpha@f$ | 0.4 | Gradient regularization coefficient |
  @endparblock

  @subsection examples-shapeopt-sc-setup-mesh Supply the mesh

  Hydrate the repository's Git LFS resources, build `SimpleCantilever2D`,
  and pass `SimpleCantilever2D.mfem.mesh`
  as the executable's input argument. Run from a scratch directory so the
  exported fields and meshes are kept separate from the sources. The mesh
  carries boundary labels one, two and three for the free, clamped and loaded
  boundary parts respectively.

  Build and run the example from a configured checkout with these commands.

  @code{.sh}
  git lfs pull --include="resources/examples/ShapeOptimization/SimpleCantilever2D.mfem.mesh"
  cmake --build build --target SimpleCantilever2D
  mkdir -p results/simple-cantilever
  cd results/simple-cantilever
  ../../build/examples/ShapeOptimization/SimpleCantilever2D \
    ../../resources/examples/ShapeOptimization/SimpleCantilever2D.mfem.mesh
  @endcode

  Check the input's boundary attributes when substituting another mesh:

  @m_class{m-scroll}

  @parblock
  | Facet attribute | Boundary | Data |
  | :--- | :--- | :--- |
  | 1 | @f$\Gamma_0@f$ | Traction-free design boundary |
  | 2 | @f$\Gamma_D@f$ | Zero elastic displacement and zero design motion |
  | 3 | @f$\Gamma_N@f$ | Downward unit traction and zero design motion |
  @endparblock

  @section examples-shapeopt-sc-loop One iteration

  @subsection examples-shapeopt-sc-loop-state Solve the elastic state

  The displacement satisfies the small-strain elasticity weak form with
  zero displacement on the clamp and a downward unit traction on the loaded
  boundary. The program checks sparse factorization and solution status.

  With @f$\varepsilon(u)=\tfrac12(\nabla u+\nabla u^T)@f$ and
  @f$\sigma(u)=2\mu\varepsilon(u)+\lambda\operatorname{tr}\varepsilon(u)I@f$,
  the weak problem is
  @f[
    \int_\Omega\sigma(u):\varepsilon(v)\,dx
       =\int_{\Gamma_N}f\cdot v\,ds,
    \qquad f=(0,-1),
  @f]
  for test fields vanishing on @f$\Gamma_D@f$. The absence of a traction
  term on @f$\Gamma_0@f$ imposes its natural zero-traction condition.

  @snippet ShapeOptimization/SimpleCantilever2D.cpp shape-state

  @subsection examples-shapeopt-sc-loop-gradient Compute a regularized descent

  Let @f$n@f$ be the outward material normal and
  @f$W=\sigma(u):\varepsilon(u)@f$. For smooth free-boundary variations
  with fixed support and load boundary, the continuous shape derivative is
  @f[
    J'(\Omega)[\xi]=\int_{\Gamma_0}(\ell-W)\,\xi\cdot n\,ds.
  @f]
  Compliance is self-adjoint, so the elastic state also supplies the adjoint
  information needed for this derivative. The program therefore uses the
  state strain in the boundary density without a separate adjoint solve.
  Its negative provides a scalar boundary load @f$W-\ell@f$. Regions with
  large @f$W@f$ relative to @f$\ell@f$ favor an outward design motion; the area
  penalty favors material removal. The regularization solve couples these
  boundary tendencies into a smooth vector field throughout the body.
  @f[
    \alpha\int_\Omega\nabla\theta:\nabla w\,dx
      +\int_\Omega\theta\cdot w\,dx
      =\int_{\Gamma_0}(W-\ell)\,n\cdot w\,ds.
  @f]
  Both @f$\theta@f$ and @f$w@f$ vanish on the clamped and loaded parts.
  The boundary derivative is assembled by `BoundaryIntegral`, and `g` is
  the trial function for @f$\theta@f$.

  @snippet ShapeOptimization/SimpleCantilever2D.cpp shape-gradient

  The gradient term in this program has coefficient @f$\alpha@f$; the
  corresponding smoothing length is @f$\sqrt{\alpha}@f$. This convention is
  explicit in the assembled expression.

  For the continuous derivative and an exact regularization solve, testing
  with @f$w=\theta@f$ yields
  @f[
    J'(\Omega)[\theta]=
      -\alpha\|\nabla\theta\|_{L^2(\Omega)}^2
      -\|\theta\|_{L^2(\Omega)}^2\leq0.
  @f]
  This is a first-order descent relation at the current domain. The finite
  element boundary evaluation, finite motion and subsequent remeshing enter
  the actual recorded objective history.

  @subsection examples-shapeopt-sc-loop-move Normalize the displacement

  The field is scaled so its sampled maximum norm gives a displacement scale
  @f$h_{\min}@f$. Non-finite norms fail the run; a zero norm ends the updates.

  With @f$m@f$ the sampled maximum of @f$|\theta|@f$, the displacement
  applied to the mesh is @f$\delta x=(h_{\min}/m)\theta@f$. This makes
  the movement scale explicit even when the derivative magnitude changes
  during optimization. The positive scaling retains the direction of the
  regularized field. The chosen @f$h_{\min}@f$ controls both the movement
  scale and MMG's lower size setting in this example.

  @subsection examples-shapeopt-sc-loop-remesh Move and remesh

  @snippet ShapeOptimization/SimpleCantilever2D.cpp shape-update

  The fixed step is a demonstration policy. Inspect the recorded objective
  history when changing meshes, loads or material parameters; an objective-
  controlled line search can be introduced for a specified optimization policy.
  New spaces and problems are constructed on each remeshed domain.

  `Omega.displace()` first moves the current mesh. MMG then changes the mesh
  as needed to meet its size and quality controls. The following iteration
  rebuilds the finite element spaces and recomputes the elastic state on that
  domain. The recorded sequence consists of a state solve, a descent solve, objective/output,
  displacement and remeshing.

  @m_class{rodin-mesh-stages}

  @parblock
  @image html SimpleCantileverStages-state.png "First computed elastic state. The mesh gives the design geometry; color shows the elastic displacement magnitude."
  @image html SimpleCantileverStages-descent.png "Computed descent at the first state. Color shows its magnitude divided by the sampled maximum; equal-length arrows show direction."
  @image html SimpleCantileverStages-displaced.png "First design displacement, before MMG. The navy outline marks the previous boundary; the displayed mesh uses the actual movement scale 0.01."
  @image html SimpleCantileverStages-remeshed.png "Mesh after MMG, as used by the second elastic state. Remeshing can change vertices and connectivity."
  @endparblock

  @section examples-shapeopt-sc-results Inspect the computed run

  @m_class{rodin-mesh-stages}

  @parblock
  @image html SimpleCantilever-initial.png "Elastic state on the initial material mesh."
  @image html SimpleCantilever-final.png "Elastic state on the last recorded material mesh, using the same color scale."
  @image html SimpleCantileverHistory.png "Objective values recorded before each boundary update."
  @endparblock

  The checked 50-state run reduced the objective from approximately 3.56181
  to 2.28789. Every recorded increment decreased the objective in this run.
  Both the elastic state and regularization solve succeeded at every state.

  The mesh coordinates in the comparison depict the evolving design domain;
  color encodes the elastic displacement magnitude. Inspect the load path
  between the loaded boundary and the clamp together with the compliance and
  area columns. A reduced weighted objective can combine increased compliance
  with reduced material area, depending on their relative changes.

  `SimpleCantilever.csv` records compliance, area and their weighted sum.
  `SimpleCantilever.xdmf` records the transient mesh, elastic displacement and
  unscaled regularized descent at the
  same iteration. Open the XDMF file in ParaView and select **Apply** to
  inspect that history.

  @m_class{m-scroll}

  @parblock
  | File | Interpretation |
  | :--- | :--- |
  | `SimpleCantilever.csv` | One row per solved state containing the iteration, compliance, area and @f$C+\ell|\Omega|@f$ |
  | `SimpleCantilever.xdmf` | Mesh, elastic **displacement** and unscaled **descent** for the same recorded states |
  | `Omega0.mesh` | Input geometry saved before the update loop |
  | `Omega.mesh` | Geometry after the most recent displacement and remeshing |
  @endparblock

  The full run records states 0 through 49, each before its geometry update.
  `Omega.mesh` after the loop contains the geometry following update 50.
  Its elastic response would require another state solve. The measured
  monotone history applies to the recorded run; an application can add
  objective-based step acceptance and a stopping test appropriate to its
  accuracy requirements.

  The intermediate figures show the first update. They are rendered from the
  exported fields by `visualize_stages.py` with
  `--mode simple`. The pre-remeshing geometry applies the exported descent
  with the same positive normalization and movement scale as the C++ update.

  @section examples-shapeopt-sc-full Complete source

  @ref ShapeOptimization/SimpleCantilever2D.cpp "SimpleCantilever2D.cpp"
  contains the input checks, solves, diagnostics and geometry updates.

  @section examples-shapeopt-sc-see-also Continue

